Lab 7: User Security and Role Management in OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Configure user domains and identities in OpenStack using Keystone.
Assign scoped roles (admin/member) to users.
Test security policies and access controls for different user roles.
Prerequisites
OpenStack Environment: A working OpenStack deployment with Keystone (Identity Service) installed.
Command Line Access: SSH access to the OpenStack controller node.
Admin Credentials: Admin username and password for OpenStack.
OpenStack CLI Tools: Installed and configured (openstack command available).
Lab Tasks
Task 1: Create and Manage User Domains
Subtask 1.1: Create a New Domain
Log in to the OpenStack controller node via SSH.
Source the admin OpenRC file to gain admin privileges:
source admin-openrc.sh
Create a new domain named lab_domain:
openstack domain create --description "Lab Domain" lab_domain
Expected Output:
+-------------+----------------------------------+
| Field         | Value                            |
+-------------+----------------------------------+
| description  | Lab Domain                       |
| enabled      | True                             |
| id           | [DOMAIN_ID]                      |
| name         | lab_domain                       |
+-------------+----------------------------------+
Subtask 1.2: List and Verify Domains
List all domains to verify the creation:
openstack domain list
Expected Outcome:
The output should include lab_domain in the list.
Task 2: Assign Scoped Roles to Users
Subtask 2.1: Create a New User
Create a user lab_user in lab_domain:
openstack user create --domain lab_domain --password-prompt lab_user
Note: You will be prompted to set a password for the user.
Subtask 2.2: Assign Admin and Member Roles
Assign the admin role to lab_user in the lab_domain:

openstack role add --domain lab_domain --user lab_user admin
Verify the role assignment:

openstack role assignment list --user lab_user --domain lab_domain
Expected Outcome:
The output should show admin role assigned to lab_user.

Repeat the process to assign the member role:

openstack role add --domain lab_domain --user lab_user member
Task 3: Configure and Test Security Policies
Subtask 3.1: Test Admin Access
Switch to the lab_user context:
source lab_user-openrc.sh
Attempt to list all projects (admin-only action):
openstack project list
Expected Outcome:
The command should succeed if lab_user has the admin role.
Subtask 3.2: Test Member Access
Remove the admin role temporarily:
openstack role remove --domain lab_domain --user lab_user admin
Retry listing projects:
openstack project list
Expected Outcome:
The command should fail with a 403 Forbidden error, indicating restricted access.
Troubleshooting Tips
Permission Denied: Ensure the user has the correct role assignments and the domain is properly configured.
Command Not Found: Verify OpenStack CLI tools are installed (pip install python-openstackclient).
Authentication Errors: Double-check credentials in the OpenRC file.
Conclusion
In this lab, you learned how to:

Create and manage user domains in OpenStack.
Assign and verify scoped roles (admin/member) to users.
Test security policies to enforce access controls.
These skills are essential for managing multi-tenant environments and ensuring proper role-based access in OpenStack deployments.


